# Vendored canonical artifacts (contracts/, content-schema/schemas/vendor/) are
# byte-exact copies verified by SHA-256 checksum against provenance.json. Any
# EOL normalization (e.g. core.autocrlf=true converting LF -> CRLF on
# checkout) silently changes their bytes without a semantic diff, breaking
# checksum verification. Mark them binary so Git never rewrites their bytes,
# regardless of the developer's global core.autocrlf setting.
packages/contracts/vendor/** -text
packages/content-schema/schemas/vendor/** -text

# Tranche E1 staging tooling (tools/*.mjs): same root cause as above, a real
# regression caught by post-merge verification, not a local quirk. On a
# Windows checkout with the common core.autocrlf=true default, these files
# are silently rewritten LF -> CRLF, which then makes Vite/esbuild's parser
# (used by `pnpm -r run test`, i.e. vitest) fail with a misleading
# "SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token" reported against whichever
# file imports the CRLF one — even though `node --check` accepts the same
# file without complaint. The committed blobs are plain LF; marking these
# files binary keeps every checkout byte-identical to that blob regardless
# of the developer's global core.autocrlf setting.
tools/backup-staging-db.mjs -text
tools/backup-staging-db.test.mjs -text
tools/restore-staging-db.mjs -text
tools/restore-staging-db.test.mjs -text
